1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,663
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,723 --> 00:00:09,911
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:10,623
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:13,168
Welcome to Unit 7: Salts.

5
00:00:14,227 --> 00:00:20,227
Salt flavours our food, keeps our bodies working, preserves food and even melts ice on winter roads.

6
00:00:20,227 --> 00:00:25,455
It's made by mining, pumping water into underground salt beds, or evaporating seawater.

7
00:00:25,455 --> 00:00:27,890
But table salt is just one of many salts!

8
00:00:28,949 --> 00:00:34,177
Table salt, sodium chloride, is just one salt. Ammonium nitrate is used in fertilisers,

9
00:00:34,177 --> 00:00:39,880
calcium sulfate in plaster casts for broken bones, and sodium hydrogen carbonate is baking soda.

10
00:00:40,939 --> 00:00:47,177
A salt is an ionic compound. Sodium chloride is made of positive sodium ions and negative chloride ions.

11
00:00:47,177 --> 00:00:49,256
Every acid contains hydrogen ions,

12
00:00:49,256 --> 00:00:54,364
and a salt forms when those hydrogen ions are replaced by metal ions or ammonium ions.

13
00:00:55,424 --> 00:01:01,245
A common way to make a salt is to react an acid with a base: acid plus base gives salt plus water.

14
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:08,364
Common acids are hydrochloric, sulfuric and nitric acid. Bases are metal oxides and metal hydroxides.

15
00:01:08,364 --> 00:01:14,245
Bases that dissolve in water are called alkalis, like sodium hydroxide; others, like copper oxide,

16
00:01:14,245 --> 00:01:16,680
are insoluble but still react with acids.

17
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:22,433
Hydrochloric acid plus potassium hydroxide gives potassium chloride and water.

18
00:01:22,433 --> 00:01:27,779
The metal comes from the base, the rest from the acid: hydrochloric acid makes chlorides,

19
00:01:27,779 --> 00:01:30,571
sulfuric makes sulfates, nitric makes nitrates.

20
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:37,927
Hydrochloric acid is neutralised exactly with sodium hydroxide solution: the indicator shows green, pH 7.

21
00:01:37,927 --> 00:01:43,808
The solution is then heated gently in an evaporating dish. Predict: what will be left in the dish?

22
00:01:43,808 --> 00:01:44,818
Pause and decide.

23
00:01:45,877 --> 00:01:49,145
Watch. As the solution is heated, the water evaporates.

24
00:01:50,204 --> 00:01:54,956
Small white crystals are left in the dish. They are sodium chloride: table salt.

25
00:01:56,016 --> 00:01:57,323
Explain what you saw.

26
00:01:57,323 --> 00:02:05,164
The acid and the alkali reacted to make a salt and water: hydrochloric acid plus sodium hydroxide gives sodium chloride plus water.

27
00:02:05,164 --> 00:02:08,253
The water evaporated, leaving the salt as crystals.

28
00:02:08,253 --> 00:02:13,183
The sodium came from the alkali, and the chloride came from the hydrochloric acid.

29
00:02:13,183 --> 00:02:15,500
Pause and explain it in your own words.
